WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:13.400
Secure input handling depends on what happens when input is wrong. Fail closed means the program rejects, stops, or falls back to a safe state when a required check fails. It does not continue with partially initialized data.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:21.540
It does not guess a default that changes the security meaning of the request. It does not log an error and keep processing as if validation succeeded.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:32.480
Consistent error returns make this easier because callers can handle one clear failure path instead of many ambiguous outcomes. Cleanup paths deserve the same attention as success paths.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:45.740
A parser that allocates memory, opens files, locks resources, or builds partial objects must release them correctly when validation fails. The cleanup code should avoid leaks, double frees, and use-after-free behavior.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:53.880
Logging is useful, but log messages should not expose secrets, raw tokens, private paths, or unbounded attacker-controlled text.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:59.060
Error messages should help operators diagnose problems without becoming another input handling problem.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:12.720
Testing should include the inputs real programs actually receive: empty input, maximum-length input, one byte over the limit, missing terminators, malformed encodings, invalid numbers, huge counts, negative values,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:24.220
truncated records, duplicate fields, and random data. Unit tests are good for known boundaries. Fuzzing is valuable because it explores combinations humans do not think to write by hand.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:35.560
Compiler warnings, sanitizers, and static analysis add another layer by surfacing memory errors, undefined behavior, suspicious conversions, and boundary mistakes during development.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:41.780
The point of testing malformed input is to prove that rejection is as engineered as acceptance.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:52.240
The example uses assert as a compact regression-test mechanism and therefore must be compiled without NDEBUG; production validation must never depend on assertions that can be compiled out.
